GCC 15.3.0, Darwin: inline GPR/FPR/VR saves and restores unless optimising
for size

rs6000_savres_strategy sends Darwin to the out-of-line saveGPR/restGPRx
(and saveFP/restFP) routines whenever more than two GPRs or four FPRs are
saved, at every optimisation level; ELF/V4 inlines them unless -Os. With
the routines, every non-leaf C++ function in JavaScriptCore pays a bl/blr
pair into a long std/ld run on entry and exit. This gives Darwin the ELF
behaviour: inline unless optimize_size; -Os keeps the out-of-line code.

Measured (FrostByte JIT work, private cc1plus in build/gccx): JSC parse
5-7.5% faster, Octane CodeLoad +9% on the T4240. Applied only to the
toolchain-user64-gcc15p userland compiler; the kernel toolchain does not
take patches from this directory.

Origin: leopard_webkitbrowser patches/toolchain-gcc15-darwin-inline-savres.patch

--- a/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000-logue.cc
+++ b/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000-logue.cc
@@ -366,6 +366,17 @@
 	    strategy |= SAVE_INLINE_VRS | REST_INLINE_VRS;
 	}
     }
+  else if (DEFAULT_ABI == ABI_DARWIN && !optimize_size)
+    {
+      /* Inline saves and restores when optimizing for speed, as for
+	 ABI_V4/ELF above.  Darwin used out-of-line saveGPR/restGPRx even at
+	 -O2; inlining them measured parse 5-7.5% and Octane CodeLoad 9%
+	 faster in JavaScriptCore on the T4240 (patch 0102).
+	 -Os keeps the out-of-line routines.  */
+      strategy |= SAVE_INLINE_FPRS | REST_INLINE_FPRS;
+      strategy |= SAVE_INLINE_GPRS | REST_INLINE_GPRS;
+      strategy |= SAVE_INLINE_VRS | REST_INLINE_VRS;
+    }
   else if (DEFAULT_ABI == ABI_DARWIN)
     {
       if (info->first_fp_reg_save > 60)
